# Iterating Over Lines in PHP: Handling Strings, Line Breaks, and SQL Generation

Handling user input that mimics a file uploadâwhether via an uploaded stream or a pasted textareaâis a common pattern in modern applications. The challenge isn't parsing the raw string itself; it's doing so robustly without relying on platform-specific newline conventions like `\r`, `\n`, or `\r\n`. In PHP, you don't need to reinvent the wheel to split this data. The `explode()` function is your primary tool, but its behavior depends entirely on how you configure the delimiter and handle edge cases.

To iterate over each line in a string while ignoring specific newline characters, you should target the carriage return (`\r`) and line feed (`\n`) explicitly. If your application runs on Linux or macOS, `\n` is sufficient. On Windows, you might encounter `\r\n`. The safest approach to normalize these differences before processing is to use `str_replace()` to convert all variations of newlines into a single standard character, such as the newline only (`\n`).

```php
$input = "Line 1\r\nLine 2\nLine 3\r"; // Mixed line endings from user input

// Normalize mixed line endings to a standard \n
$normalizedInput = str_replace(["\r\n", "\r"], "\n", $input);

// Split the string into an array of lines
$lines = explode("\n", $normalizedInput);
```

Once you have the array, iterating is straightforward using `foreach`. However, a critical pitfall arises when processing the last element of the split string. The `explode()` function retains an empty string at the end of the array if the input ends with a newline character. If your user pastes "Item A\n", the resulting array will contain `['Item A', '']`. You must filter out this trailing empty element to prevent generating invalid SQL queries or processing null values.

```php
// Remove trailing empty lines caused by split on final newline
$lines = array_values(array_filter($lines, fn($line) => trim($line) !== ''));
```

After splitting and filtering, the next step is validation. Your requirement specifies exactly one token per line with no spaces, tabs, or commas. A naive `trim()` won't catch internal whitespace. You need to check if the trimmed string matches a pattern that ensures it contains only alphanumeric characters (or specific allowed symbols) and has a length greater than zero.

```php
$validLines = [];

foreach ($lines as $line) {
    // Trim leading/trailing whitespace
    $trimmedLine = trim($line);

    // Skip if empty after trimming
    if (empty($trimmedLine)) {
        continue;
    }

    // Strict validation: exactly one token, no spaces/tabs/commas
    // Using preg_match to ensure the entire string is one "word"
    if (preg_match('/^\w+$/', $trimmedLine)) {
        // Sanitize here: e.g., remove special chars not in \w or map to safe enum IDs
        $sanitized = strtolower($trimmedLine); 
        $validLines[] = $sanitized;
    }
}
```

Generating SQL queries based on this list requires careful attention to security. Never concatenate raw user input directly into a query string, even after basic trimming. Use prepared statements via PDO or the database abstraction layer provided by your framework. If you are using Laravel, the `DB` facade handles this seamlessly with batch inserts:

```php
// Assuming $validLines contains sanitized, safe identifiers
$ids = implode(',', array_map(fn($val) => "'$val'", $validLines));

$sql = "SELECT * FROM table WHERE id IN ($ids)"; // Only if IDs are strictly validated enums/numbers
```

The `array_values()` function is essential here to reindex the array numerically after removing empty elements, ensuring your loop indices remain consistent. Additionally, consider using PHP's built-in stream wrappers or file handling functions if the input volume becomes massive; reading a string from a textarea into memory can be inefficient for gigabyte-sized datasets, though `explode` handles typical text blocks effortlessly.

If you are building this logic within a Laravel application, you might leverage existing packages or artisan commands that handle CSV parsing or bulk imports, but native PHP functions offer the granular control needed to enforce strict token validation before data ever reaches your database layer.
